1
00:00:00,310 --> 00:00:02,180
November 1918.

2
00:00:02,210 --> 00:00:05,020
A forest near Compiègne,
in northern France.

3
00:00:05,050 --> 00:00:09,165
Inside a railway carriage, German
officials sign the armistice.

4
00:00:09,485 --> 00:00:12,805
The First World War is over
— and Germany has lost.

5
00:00:12,925 --> 00:00:17,320
Twenty-two years later, in June
1940, Adolf Hitler orders that same

6
00:00:17,350 --> 00:00:21,905
carriage pulled out of its museum
and placed on the exact same spot.

7
00:00:22,335 --> 00:00:24,625
This time, it is France that signs.

8
00:00:24,655 --> 00:00:29,903
In twenty-two years, Germany went from total
defeat to ruling almost all of Europe.

9
00:00:30,130 --> 00:00:31,740
And yet, five years after that,

10
00:00:31,770 --> 00:00:35,505
Hitler was dead in a bunker
and Germany lay in ruins.

11
00:00:35,775 --> 00:00:40,075
Somewhere between those two moments,
Hitler lost the Second World War.

12
00:00:40,355 --> 00:00:41,835
Not in 1945.

13
00:00:41,865 --> 00:00:43,395
Much earlier.

14
00:00:43,430 --> 00:00:45,635
By the end of this video,
you'll know the exact

15
00:00:45,665 --> 00:00:48,840
day it happened — and why
he never saw it coming.

16
00:00:49,140 --> 00:00:52,180
To find that day, we have
to solve five questions.

17
00:00:52,345 --> 00:00:54,405
One: how did a country that wasn't allowed

18
00:00:54,430 --> 00:00:57,445
an army build the most
feared army in Europe?

19
00:00:57,690 --> 00:00:59,125
Two: why did nobody stop it?

20
00:00:59,155 --> 00:01:01,345
Three: how did France
fall in just six weeks?

21
00:01:01,375 --> 00:01:03,898
Four: why didn't Britain give up?

22
00:01:03,928 --> 00:01:08,923
And five: why did Hitler attack the Soviet
Union while Britain was still fighting?

23
00:01:09,235 --> 00:01:11,278
Along the way, we'll pick up three clues.

24
00:01:11,308 --> 00:01:13,943
Put them together — and
you'll have the answer.

25
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:15,540
Question one.

26
00:01:15,570 --> 00:01:18,813
How do you build an army when
you're not allowed to have one?

27
00:01:18,990 --> 00:01:22,993
After 1918, the Treaty of
Versailles cut Germany down.

28
00:01:23,145 --> 00:01:25,707
Its army was capped at
one hundred thousand men.

29
00:01:25,737 --> 00:01:26,388
No tanks.

30
00:01:26,418 --> 00:01:27,347
No air force.

31
00:01:27,377 --> 00:01:28,972
No submarines.

32
00:01:29,265 --> 00:01:33,645
Germany had to accept blame for
the war and pay huge reparations.

33
00:01:33,890 --> 00:01:36,440
Millions of Germans felt humiliated.

34
00:01:36,470 --> 00:01:39,435
One of them was a
twenty-nine-year-old corporal.

35
00:01:39,510 --> 00:01:44,710
He learned of the surrender in a military
hospital, temporarily blinded by gas.

36
00:01:45,035 --> 00:01:46,960
His name was Adolf Hitler.

37
00:01:47,060 --> 00:01:53,460
Fifteen years later, in January 1933, that
corporal became Chancellor of Germany.

38
00:01:53,715 --> 00:01:55,895
Within months, he had crushed every rival

39
00:01:55,925 --> 00:01:59,140
party and turned Germany
into a dictatorship.

40
00:01:59,345 --> 00:02:02,218
Then he began breaking the
treaty — step by step.

41
00:02:02,345 --> 00:02:08,325
In 1935, he openly announced conscription
and a new air force: the Luftwaffe.

42
00:02:08,525 --> 00:02:09,980
Britain and France protested.

43
00:02:10,010 --> 00:02:11,525
And did nothing.

44
00:02:11,630 --> 00:02:15,190
In March 1936, he made
his biggest bet yet.

45
00:02:15,335 --> 00:02:18,767
German troops marched into the
Rhineland — a border zone next

46
00:02:18,793 --> 00:02:22,467
to France that the treaty said
must stay free of soldiers.

47
00:02:22,855 --> 00:02:25,203
The French army was still far stronger.

48
00:02:25,233 --> 00:02:28,227
If France had marched in,
it would have been over.

49
00:02:28,455 --> 00:02:33,170
Years later, Hitler's interpreter recalled
him saying: "The forty-eight hours

50
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:37,375
after the march into the Rhineland were
the most nerve-racking in my life.

51
00:02:37,605 --> 00:02:39,887
If the French had then
marched in, we would have

52
00:02:39,912 --> 00:02:43,127
had to withdraw with our
tails between our legs."

53
00:02:43,410 --> 00:02:45,050
France didn't march.

54
00:02:45,125 --> 00:02:48,690
So here's the answer to question
one: Hitler built his army

55
00:02:48,720 --> 00:02:52,315
by betting that nobody would
stop him — and winning the bet.

56
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:57,162
And every time he won, he learned the
same lesson: bold gambles pay off.

57
00:02:57,355 --> 00:02:59,027
Remember that lesson.

58
00:02:59,145 --> 00:03:00,727
It's our first clue.

59
00:03:00,780 --> 00:03:02,800
But one question remained.

60
00:03:02,830 --> 00:03:05,455
Why were Britain and
France letting him do it?

61
00:03:05,485 --> 00:03:06,995
Question two.

62
00:03:07,025 --> 00:03:08,862
Why did nobody stop him?

63
00:03:08,965 --> 00:03:13,202
To understand this, remember what Britain
and France had just lived through.

64
00:03:13,475 --> 00:03:16,982
The First World War had killed
millions of their young men.

65
00:03:17,125 --> 00:03:19,490
Almost every family had lost someone.

66
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:22,895
Leaders and voters were
terrified of doing it again.

67
00:03:23,095 --> 00:03:27,672
So when Hitler made demands, they tried to
give him just enough to keep the peace.

68
00:03:27,905 --> 00:03:30,330
This policy had a name: appeasement.

69
00:03:30,360 --> 00:03:33,805
In March 1938, Hitler annexed Austria.

70
00:03:33,895 --> 00:03:34,620
No war.

71
00:03:34,650 --> 00:03:36,520
Then he demanded the Sudetenland —

72
00:03:36,550 --> 00:03:40,245
the German-speaking border
regions of Czechoslovakia.

73
00:03:40,465 --> 00:03:43,720
In September 1938, at a
conference in Munich,

74
00:03:43,750 --> 00:03:46,665
Britain and France agreed to hand it over.

75
00:03:46,955 --> 00:03:49,542
Czechoslovakia wasn't even in the room.

76
00:03:49,675 --> 00:03:52,698
British Prime Minister Neville
Chamberlain flew home and

77
00:03:52,728 --> 00:03:56,203
told cheering crowds he had
brought "peace for our time."

78
00:03:56,475 --> 00:03:58,672
It lasted less than six months.

79
00:03:58,735 --> 00:04:03,120
In March 1939, Hitler took
the rest of Czechoslovakia.

80
00:04:03,230 --> 00:04:05,735
Now it was clear: his
promises meant nothing.

81
00:04:05,765 --> 00:04:09,668
Britain and France pledged to
defend his next target — Poland.

82
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:12,902
And then Hitler made a
move nobody expected.

83
00:04:12,980 --> 00:04:16,385
In August 1939, Nazi Germany signed a pact

84
00:04:16,415 --> 00:04:19,870
with its sworn enemy —
Stalin's Soviet Union.

85
00:04:20,225 --> 00:04:23,210
In public, they promised
not to attack each other.

86
00:04:23,300 --> 00:04:27,043
In secret, they agreed to divide
Eastern Europe between them.

87
00:04:27,175 --> 00:04:31,663
On the first of September
1939, Germany invaded Poland.

88
00:04:31,900 --> 00:04:34,790
Two days later, Britain
and France declared war.

89
00:04:34,820 --> 00:04:39,110
On the seventeenth, the Soviet
army invaded Poland from the east.

90
00:04:39,445 --> 00:04:41,510
Within weeks, Poland was gone.

91
00:04:41,540 --> 00:04:44,985
Answer to question two:
nobody stopped Hitler because

92
00:04:45,015 --> 00:04:48,790
everyone feared another world
war more than they feared him.

93
00:04:49,090 --> 00:04:51,550
By the time they understood,
it was too late.

94
00:04:51,625 --> 00:04:54,265
But France had one of the
largest armies in Europe,

95
00:04:54,295 --> 00:04:56,970
and Britain the largest navy in the world.

96
00:04:57,240 --> 00:05:00,288
Surely, this time, Hitler
had gambled too far?

97
00:05:00,340 --> 00:05:01,853
Question three.

98
00:05:01,910 --> 00:05:03,873
How did France — with one of the strongest

99
00:05:03,903 --> 00:05:06,918
armies in the world —
fall in just six weeks?

100
00:05:07,210 --> 00:05:11,080
For eight months after Poland,
almost nothing happened in the West.

101
00:05:11,285 --> 00:05:13,505
Soldiers waited in trenches and forts.

102
00:05:13,535 --> 00:05:15,670
People called it the Phoney War.

103
00:05:15,900 --> 00:05:17,525
France felt safe.

104
00:05:17,570 --> 00:05:20,877
Along its border with Germany
it had built the Maginot Line —

105
00:05:20,907 --> 00:05:23,962
a chain of massive concrete fortresses.

106
00:05:24,265 --> 00:05:27,880
Together, France and Britain
had more tanks than Germany.

107
00:05:27,995 --> 00:05:29,945
On paper, they should have won.

108
00:05:29,990 --> 00:05:33,205
But the German plan didn't
attack the Maginot Line.

109
00:05:33,365 --> 00:05:34,895
It went around it.

110
00:05:34,925 --> 00:05:39,585
On the tenth of May 1940, Germany
attacked the Netherlands and Belgium.

111
00:05:39,820 --> 00:05:42,240
The best British and
French troops rushed north

112
00:05:42,270 --> 00:05:45,705
to meet them — exactly as
the Germans had hoped.

113
00:05:45,955 --> 00:05:49,330
Because the real blow came further
south, through the Ardennes:

114
00:05:49,555 --> 00:05:53,523
hills and thick forest the French
believed tanks could hardly cross.

115
00:05:53,670 --> 00:05:58,563
German panzer columns squeezed through the
forest roads and broke through at Sedan.

116
00:05:58,725 --> 00:06:02,652
Then, instead of stopping to wait
for the infantry, they raced west.

117
00:06:02,880 --> 00:06:05,210
By the twentieth of May —
just ten days after the

118
00:06:05,240 --> 00:06:08,175
attack began — they reached
the English Channel.

119
00:06:08,445 --> 00:06:11,355
The best Allied armies
were cut off in the north.

120
00:06:11,415 --> 00:06:15,273
This speed would give German warfare
its famous name: Blitzkrieg.

121
00:06:15,303 --> 00:06:16,665
Lightning war.

122
00:06:16,695 --> 00:06:18,492
On the very day the attack began,

123
00:06:18,522 --> 00:06:22,043
Britain got a new Prime
Minister: Winston Churchill.

124
00:06:22,073 --> 00:06:22,963
Remember him.

125
00:06:22,993 --> 00:06:26,308
He's about to become the
problem Hitler can't solve.

126
00:06:26,530 --> 00:06:28,668
By mid-June, Paris had fallen.

127
00:06:28,775 --> 00:06:33,108
And on the twenty-second of June
1940, Hitler took his revenge.

128
00:06:33,350 --> 00:06:37,488
The old railway carriage was brought
back to the forest — the same carriage,

129
00:06:37,720 --> 00:06:41,228
the same spot — and France
signed the armistice inside it.

130
00:06:41,310 --> 00:06:42,937
Remember that date.

131
00:06:42,990 --> 00:06:44,132
Twenty-second of June.

132
00:06:44,162 --> 00:06:45,937
It's our second clue.

133
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:50,007
Answer to question three: France
fell because Germany struck where

134
00:06:50,037 --> 00:06:54,532
France didn't expect — and moved
faster than anyone thought possible.

135
00:06:54,825 --> 00:06:57,600
Hitler now controlled
most of Western Europe.

136
00:06:57,685 --> 00:06:59,083
Only Britain was left.

137
00:06:59,113 --> 00:07:02,467
And Hitler expected it to
ask for peace within weeks.

138
00:07:02,680 --> 00:07:03,930
It didn't.

139
00:07:03,960 --> 00:07:05,030
Why?

140
00:07:05,060 --> 00:07:06,562
Question four.

141
00:07:06,625 --> 00:07:08,905
Germany had beaten France in six weeks.

142
00:07:08,935 --> 00:07:10,810
Why didn't Britain give up?

143
00:07:10,990 --> 00:07:13,670
The first reason was a miracle on a beach.

144
00:07:13,820 --> 00:07:17,472
As German tanks closed in,
hundreds of thousands of British

145
00:07:17,502 --> 00:07:21,257
and French soldiers were trapped
around the port of Dunkirk.

146
00:07:21,670 --> 00:07:25,145
Then, on the twenty-fourth of
May, the German tanks stopped.

147
00:07:25,305 --> 00:07:27,258
Hitler had approved a halt order.

148
00:07:27,288 --> 00:07:32,242
Historians still argue about why — but
those few days gave the Allies time.

149
00:07:32,500 --> 00:07:34,752
Warships, ferries, fishing
boats and pleasure

150
00:07:34,778 --> 00:07:37,533
yachts crossed the
Channel again and again.

151
00:07:37,735 --> 00:07:40,033
By the fourth of June,
around three hundred and

152
00:07:40,063 --> 00:07:43,578
thirty-eight thousand soldiers
had been carried to England.

153
00:07:43,885 --> 00:07:46,923
Britain lost its equipment
— but it kept its army.

154
00:07:47,115 --> 00:07:51,135
That same day, Churchill told Parliament:
"We shall fight on the beaches…

155
00:07:51,165 --> 00:07:53,020
we shall never surrender."

156
00:07:53,190 --> 00:07:55,240
The second reason was in the sky.

157
00:07:55,385 --> 00:07:59,200
To invade Britain, Germany first
needed control of the air.

158
00:07:59,345 --> 00:08:02,003
In the summer of 1940, the Luftwaffe threw

159
00:08:02,033 --> 00:08:05,048
its bombers and fighters
at southern England.

160
00:08:05,345 --> 00:08:07,320
This was the Battle of Britain.

161
00:08:07,350 --> 00:08:10,820
But Britain had a secret weapon: radar.

162
00:08:10,850 --> 00:08:14,130
A chain of stations along the
coast could see German planes

163
00:08:14,160 --> 00:08:18,175
coming and send British fighters
exactly where they were needed.

164
00:08:18,475 --> 00:08:22,960
German pilots kept running into fighters
that seemed to be waiting for them.

165
00:08:23,165 --> 00:08:28,655
Then, in September, Germany switched from
attacking RAF airfields to bombing London.

166
00:08:28,915 --> 00:08:30,740
It was a nightmare for Londoners —

167
00:08:30,770 --> 00:08:34,105
but it gave the RAF the
breathing space it needed.

168
00:08:34,315 --> 00:08:39,893
On the seventeenth of September 1940,
Hitler postponed the invasion of Britain.

169
00:08:40,095 --> 00:08:41,595
It never came.

170
00:08:41,625 --> 00:08:44,575
Churchill said of the pilots:
"Never in the field of

171
00:08:44,605 --> 00:08:48,740
human conflict was so much
owed by so many to so few."

172
00:08:48,990 --> 00:08:54,505
Answer to question four: Britain didn't give
up because it saved its army at Dunkirk,

173
00:08:54,690 --> 00:08:58,813
won the battle in the sky, and had
a leader who refused to make peace.

174
00:08:58,980 --> 00:09:02,118
And for the first time,
Hitler's gamble had failed.

175
00:09:02,185 --> 00:09:03,840
That's our third clue.

176
00:09:03,905 --> 00:09:06,120
He was stuck in a war he couldn't end.

177
00:09:06,255 --> 00:09:07,695
So he did what he always did.

178
00:09:07,725 --> 00:09:09,440
He raised the stakes.

179
00:09:09,550 --> 00:09:11,085
Question five.

180
00:09:11,115 --> 00:09:15,500
Why would Hitler attack the Soviet Union
— while Britain was still fighting?

181
00:09:15,695 --> 00:09:19,142
For Hitler, the war in the
East was never just strategy.

182
00:09:19,350 --> 00:09:24,382
For years he had written and spoken about
seizing "living space" in Eastern Europe,

183
00:09:24,610 --> 00:09:27,875
and about destroying what he
called "Jewish Bolshevism."

184
00:09:27,905 --> 00:09:31,260
The pact with Stalin was
only ever temporary.

185
00:09:31,480 --> 00:09:34,015
But there was also a cold calculation.

186
00:09:34,070 --> 00:09:36,905
Hitler believed Britain was
holding on because it hoped

187
00:09:36,935 --> 00:09:40,950
the Soviet Union, and later
America, would join the war.

188
00:09:41,380 --> 00:09:46,185
Crush the Soviet Union quickly, he reasoned,
and Britain would have no hope left.

189
00:09:46,385 --> 00:09:49,570
After Poland and France, he
— and many of his generals —

190
00:09:49,600 --> 00:09:53,095
believed the Soviet Union
would fall just as fast.

191
00:09:53,430 --> 00:09:54,070
A few months.

192
00:09:54,100 --> 00:09:55,580
Before winter.

193
00:09:55,610 --> 00:09:59,530
On the twenty-second of June
1941 — exactly one year to the

194
00:09:59,555 --> 00:10:02,625
day after France signed in
that railway carriage —

195
00:10:02,655 --> 00:10:07,090
more than three million German soldiers
crossed into the Soviet Union.

196
00:10:07,575 --> 00:10:09,470
Operation Barbarossa.

197
00:10:09,500 --> 00:10:12,505
At first, it looked like
another lightning victory.

198
00:10:12,620 --> 00:10:14,900
Whole Soviet armies were surrounded.

199
00:10:14,930 --> 00:10:17,293
In September, Kyiv fell, and hundreds of

200
00:10:17,323 --> 00:10:20,558
thousands of Soviet soldiers
were taken prisoner.

201
00:10:20,865 --> 00:10:23,238
But the Soviet Union didn't collapse.

202
00:10:23,355 --> 00:10:24,373
It was enormous.

203
00:10:24,403 --> 00:10:27,938
Every time one army was
destroyed, another appeared.

204
00:10:28,110 --> 00:10:31,818
Factories were moved east, beyond
the reach of German bombers.

205
00:10:32,030 --> 00:10:34,622
Then came the rain, the
mud — and the winter.

206
00:10:34,700 --> 00:10:37,450
German soldiers had no
proper winter clothing,

207
00:10:37,480 --> 00:10:40,375
because no one had planned
to still be fighting.

208
00:10:40,565 --> 00:10:45,445
By December, German troops were only
a few dozen kilometres from Moscow.

209
00:10:45,725 --> 00:10:50,515
Then, on the fifth of December
1941, the Soviet army struck back.

210
00:10:50,785 --> 00:10:54,415
For the first time, the German
army was thrown into retreat.

211
00:10:54,585 --> 00:10:58,408
Six days later, Hitler declared
war on the United States.

212
00:10:58,580 --> 00:11:02,303
Answer to question five: Hitler
attacked the Soviet Union

213
00:11:02,333 --> 00:11:06,168
because he believed one more
lightning victory would end the war.

214
00:11:06,530 --> 00:11:08,787
Instead, he now faced the British Empire,

215
00:11:08,817 --> 00:11:12,453
the Soviet Union and the
United States — all at once.

216
00:11:12,770 --> 00:11:15,718
So — when did Hitler lose
the Second World War?

217
00:11:15,905 --> 00:11:19,315
Not in 1945, in the ruins of Berlin.

218
00:11:19,425 --> 00:11:21,240
Not even at Stalingrad.

219
00:11:21,270 --> 00:11:23,005
Look at our three clues.

220
00:11:23,100 --> 00:11:27,275
One: every gamble paid off — the Rhineland,
Austria, Czechoslovakia, France.

221
00:11:27,305 --> 00:11:32,288
Two: the twenty-second of June — his
greatest triumph, in that railway carriage.

222
00:11:32,318 --> 00:11:35,860
Three: Britain — the first gamble he lost.

223
00:11:35,890 --> 00:11:39,263
And when that gamble failed, he
made the biggest bet of all.

224
00:11:39,420 --> 00:11:43,313
Hitler lost the war on the
twenty-second of June 1941 —

225
00:11:43,343 --> 00:11:46,678
one year to the day after
his triumph in the carriage.

226
00:11:47,075 --> 00:11:49,313
The victory that made
him master of Europe is

227
00:11:49,343 --> 00:11:52,578
exactly what convinced
him he could not lose.

228
00:11:52,895 --> 00:11:54,997
Germany could win short wars.

229
00:11:55,065 --> 00:11:59,323
It could never win a long war against
the three greatest powers on earth.

230
00:11:59,485 --> 00:12:05,418
On the thirtieth of April 1945, Hitler
took his own life in his bunker in Berlin.

231
00:12:05,660 --> 00:12:08,063
Eight days later, Germany surrendered.

232
00:12:08,130 --> 00:12:09,843
And the railway carriage?

233
00:12:09,910 --> 00:12:12,215
It was taken to Germany as a trophy.

234
00:12:12,245 --> 00:12:15,255
In 1945, it was destroyed.

235
00:12:15,415 --> 00:12:19,655
Hitler's war ended the way
his story began: in defeat.

236
00:12:19,685 --> 00:12:23,148
But if Hitler lost the war
in 1941, why did it take

237
00:12:23,178 --> 00:12:26,772
four more years — and millions
more lives — to end it?

238
00:12:27,145 --> 00:12:28,100
That's the next story.

239
00:12:28,130 --> 00:12:29,745
It's on your screen now.

